TITLE:: RLExtension
summary:: Chords as elements of a group: the extensions of the roots by the chord types, with a movable base chord
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLChordContext, Classes/RLWreath, Classes/RLChordSpace, Classes/RLJust, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
Once a strong::base chord:: is chosen, the chord whose root is z above the base's and whose type differs by h is the element code::[z, h]::. The groups that move the chords simply transitively, contain the transpositions and respect the types are the extensions of the roots by the types (Popoff, after the blog's "neo-Riemannian theory" 7 and 8). Their elements multiply as

code::
[z1, h1] [z2, h2] = [z1 + phi(h1)(z2) + zeta(h1, h2), h1 h2]
::

with phi(h) an automorphism of the roots for each type element h, and zeta a pitch class for each pair of them. Multiplying a chord's element on the strong::left:: moves every chord by the same rule; on the strong::right::, the move is read from the chord (contextual). The two commute.

There are three ways in, from the particular to the general.

strong::Two types, an equal division:: code::RLExtension(n, p, q)::. phi(1) multiplies by q and zeta(1, 1) is p; a group when q q = 1 and p (q - 1) = 0 modulo n. In 12 steps there are eight, told apart by the orders of their elements:

table::
## (p, q) || the group ||
## (0, 11) || dihedral, of order 24 || left: T and I; right: P, L, R (link::Classes/RLChordContext::)
## (0, 1) || Z12 x Z2 || no contextual action
## (1, 1) || cyclic, of order 24 ||
## (0, 5) || Z4 x S3 ||
## (0, 7) || Z3 x D8 ||
## (2, 7) || Z3 x Q8 ||
## (3, 5) || Z3 : Z8 ||
## (6, 11) || Z3 : Q8 ||
::

A prime division (19, 31) has two, the cyclic and the dihedral: the richness of 12 comes from 12 not being prime.

strong::Any number of types in a cycle, any pitch system:: code::RLExtension.cyclic(system, types, p, q)::. phi(h) is q taken h times, and zeta(h, k) is p when h + k passes the number of types. q is a multiplier, or on a just lattice a matrix; a group when q taken code::types:: times is the identity and q keeps p. On the lattice of link::Classes/RLJust:: with q = -1 this is the group of T, I, P, L, R on just triads; other matrices give other groups there (thirds and fifths exchanged, for one).

strong::Any finite group of types:: code::RLExtension.general(system, table, phi, zeta)::, the type group given by its table. The 48 affine maps of twelve pitch classes are Z12 by the four-group of its multipliers 1, 5, 7, 11, moving four types of chords.

In each case the data are checked (phi sends products to composites, zeta is a cocycle that is zero on the identity) and refused when they give no group. With code::strict: false:: they are taken as they are; the product is then not associative and the two actions no longer commute (the ChucK piece of 2019 ran such structures by a swap of parameters: a record, not a method).

The base chord is an argument of every action: moving it changes which element each chord is, so the same element then acts differently.

code::
~space = RLChordSpace.triads(RLEDO(12));
~base = ~space.major(0);
~ext = RLExtension(12, 2, 7);
~ext.orders;                                    // [[1, 1], [2, 1], [3, 2], [4, 6], [6, 2], [12, 12]]: Z3 x Q8
~ext.right([3, 1], ~space.minor(7), ~base);     // read from the chord
~ext.left([3, 1], ~space.minor(7), ~base);      // the same rule for every chord
~ext.right([3, 1], ~space.minor(7), ~space.minor(0));   // another base, another chord
RLExtension.classes(12);                        // one (p, q) for each of the eight groups
RLExtension.classes(19);                        // [[0, 1], [0, 18]]

// three types in a cycle on 7 steps: the group of order 21
RLExtension.cyclic(RLEDO(7), 3, 0, 2).orders;   // [[1, 1], [3, 14], [7, 6]]

// the just lattice
~just = RLJust([3, 5]);  ~jspace = RLChordSpace.triads(~just);
~d = RLExtension.cyclic(~just);                 // q = -1: T, I on the left, P, L, R on the right
~d.right([[0, 1], 1], ~jspace.major, ~jspace.major);          // 5/4 minor: L
~swap = RLExtension.cyclic(~just, 2, [1, 1], [[0, 1], [1, 0]]);   // thirds and fifths exchanged, p = 15/8
~swap.right([[0, 0], 1], ~jspace.minor, ~jspace.major);       // 15/8 major

// any group of types: the 48 affine maps, on four types
~klein = [[0, 1, 2, 3], [1, 0, 3, 2], [2, 3, 0, 1], [3, 2, 1, 0]];
~hol = RLExtension.general(RLEDO(12), ~klein, [1, 11, 5, 7], { 0 });
~hol.elements.size;                             // 48

RLExtension(12, 7, 0);                          // an Error: not a group
RLExtension(12, 7, 0, false).failures;          // taken as it is: 3168 triples fail (a b) c = a (b c)
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Two types on an equal division of n steps.
argument:: n
argument:: p
argument:: q
n - 1 (the dihedral group) when nil.
argument:: strict
true: data that give no group are an Error. false: they are taken as they are.

METHOD:: cyclic
argument:: system
A link::Classes/RLPitchSystem:: for the roots.
argument:: types
How many chord types, permuted in a cycle.
argument:: p
A pitch class of the system; its zero when nil.
argument:: q
An Integer multiplier, or for a lattice a matrix (an Array of rows); -1 when nil.
argument:: strict

METHOD:: general
argument:: system
argument:: table
The type group: code::table[h][k]:: is the product of the type elements h and k, 0 the identity.
argument:: phi
One automorphism of the roots per type element: multipliers, or matrices on a lattice.
argument:: zeta
code::zeta[h][k]::, a pitch class for each pair, or a Function code::(h, k) -> pitch class::.
argument:: strict

discussion::
strong::Roots that do not commute.:: code::system:: may be an link::Classes/RLTableGroup::: phi is then a list of automorphisms, each the images of the elements, and zeta a table of elements. The product is the same, code::[n1 phi(h1)(n2) zeta(h1, h2), h1 h2]::, and the conditions for a group are Schreier's: each phi(h) an automorphism, phi(h) phi(k) equal to phi(h k) followed by the conjugation by zeta(h, k), and zeta a cocycle. An element is then no chord (link::#-element:: and the actions on chords do not apply): the group acts on its own elements. See link::Classes/RLTableGroup:: for the 48 transformations of 24 quarter-tones as T and I of 12 notes by two types.

METHOD:: all
Every extension of an equal division's roots by a group of types (its table): one for each action and each class of cocycles. Two cocycles that differ by a coboundary give one group, read from other base points, so one of each class is kept.
code::
RLExtension.all(RLEDO(12), [[0, 1], [1, 0]]).collect(_.orders);      // the eight groups of 12 steps and two types
RLExtension.all(RLEDO(4), [[0, 1], [1, 0]]).size;                    // 4: Z4 x Z2, Z8, the dihedral and the quaternion group
RLExtension.all(RLEDO(8), [[0, 1], [1, 0]]).size;                    // 6, of which two have the same element orders
::

METHOD:: actions
Every way a group of types can act on the roots of an equal division: one multiplier for each type element, products going to products.

METHOD:: cocycles
Every cocycle for an action: the lists code::zeta[h][k]::, zero on the identity, with phi(h)(zeta(k, l)) + zeta(h, k l) = zeta(h, k) + zeta(h k, l). An Error past code::max:: of them.

METHOD:: isValid
Whether (p, q) gives a group on n steps with types in a cycle (two when not said).

METHOD:: valid
Every valid pair code::[p, q]::.

METHOD:: classes
One pair for each group: the first of each set of pairs whose elements have the same orders (enough to tell the groups apart for two types in 12 steps).

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: system
METHOD:: table
METHOD:: types
The pitch system of the roots; the type group's table; the number of types.

METHOD:: n
METHOD:: p
METHOD:: q
The steps of an equal division (nil on a lattice); the two parameters of a cyclic extension (nil for a general one).

METHOD:: isGroup
Whether the data give a group.

METHOD:: identity
METHOD:: elements
The element that moves nothing; every element of a finite system (an Error on a lattice).

METHOD:: mul
The product of two elements.

METHOD:: inv
The element that undoes one on both sides, or nil.

METHOD:: pow
METHOD:: order
An element taken k times; how many times before the identity (nil when never).

METHOD:: orders
How many elements have each order, as code::[order, count]:: pairs.

METHOD:: failures
METHOD:: isAssociative
Over every triple of elements of a finite system: how many fail (a b) c = a (b c); whether none does.

METHOD:: element
METHOD:: chordOf
A chord as an element, given the base chord; and back.

METHOD:: left
METHOD:: right
A chord multiplied by an element on the left, on the right.
argument:: g
An element code::[z, h]::.
argument:: chord
argument:: base
The base chord: a chord of the same space, which has as many types as the extension.
